{"rows":[{"n":1,"file":"topics/01-java-core-jvm.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":2,"file":"topics/02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":3,"file":"topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":4,"file":"topics/04-rest-grpc-webflux.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":5,"file":"topics/05-db-core-index-lock.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":6,"file":"topics/06-db-scaling.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":7,"file":"topics/07-sql-nosql-db-engines.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":8,"file":"topics/08-message-queue.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":9,"file":"topics/09-distributed-tx-fintech.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":12,"file":"topics/12-architecture-patterns.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":13,"file":"topics/13-security-oauth2.json","topic_type":"platform"},{"n":14,"file":"topics/14-devops-k8s-best-practices.json","topic_type":"platform"},{"n":15,"file":"topics/15-network-i-o-models.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":16,"file":"topics/16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.json","topic_type":"algorithm","system_design_items":["16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q1","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q2","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q3","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q4","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q5","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.whiteboard-dsa.q1"]},{"n":17,"file":"topics/17-rest-api-design.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":18,"file":"topics/18-query-optimization.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":19,"file":"topics/19-dsa-leetcode.json","topic_type":"algorithm"},{"n":20,"file":"topics/20-observability-sre.json","topic_type":"platform"},{"n":21,"file":"topics/21-linux-production-debug.json","topic_type":"platform"},{"n":22,"file":"topics/22-low-level-design-ood.json","topic_type":"algorithm"},{"n":23,"file":"topics/23-java-concurrency-coding.json","topic_type":"algorithm"},{"n":24,"file":"topics/24-domain-driven-design.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":25,"topic_type":"microservice","file":"topics/25-microservice.json"},{"n":26,"file":"topics/26-testing-strategy.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":28,"file":"topics/28-distributed-lock-lease.json","topic_type":"design"}],"row":{"n":13,"file":"topics/13-security-oauth2.json","topic_type":"platform"},"meta":{"topic_type":"platform","key":"13-security-oauth2","vi":{"label":"Security & OAuth2","title":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & thiết kế hệ auth","intro":"Auth là phần không thể sai trong fintech. Nắm vững OAuth2/OIDC, cấu trúc & xác thực JWT, cách thiết kế xác thực/uỷ quyền cho micro-services — rồi tới phần code chạm input không tin cậy (injection, mass assignment, deserialization, SSRF) và bảo vệ dữ liệu đã lưu.","tags":["OAuth2 grant","PKCE","OIDC","JWT","Access/Refresh","mTLS","OWASP","Secrets","Keycloak","Injection","Mass assignment","SSRF","Data protection","Supply chain"]},"en":{"label":"Security & OAuth2","title":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","intro":"Auth is the part you cannot get wrong in fintech. Know OAuth2/OIDC, how a JWT is built and verified, and how to design authentication and authorisation across micro-services — then the code that touches untrusted input (injection, mass assignment, deserialization, SSRF) and how stored data is protected.","tags":["OAuth2 grant","PKCE","OIDC","JWT","Access/Refresh","mTLS","OWASP","Secrets","Keycloak","Injection","Mass assignment","SSRF","Data protection","Supply chain"]},"created_at":"2026-07-31","updated_at":"2026-10-26"},"topicMeta":{"1":{"en":{"label":"Java Core & JVM"},"vi":{"label":"Java Core & JVM"}},"2":{"en":{"label":"Java 8→25 & Java vs Go"},"vi":{"label":"Java 8→25 & Java vs Go"}},"3":{"en":{"label":"Spring Boot deep dive + build"},"vi":{"label":"Spring Boot deep + build"}},"4":{"en":{"label":"REST/gRPC & WebFlux"},"vi":{"label":"REST/gRPC & WebFlux"}},"5":{"en":{"label":"DB core: indexes & locking"},"vi":{"label":"DB core: index & lock"}},"6":{"en":{"label":"DB scaling"},"vi":{"label":"DB scaling"}},"7":{"en":{"label":"SQL/NoSQL & DB engines"},"vi":{"label":"SQL/NoSQL & DB engines"}},"8":{"en":{"label":"Message queue"},"vi":{"label":"Message queue"}},"9":{"en":{"label":"Distributed tx (fintech)"},"vi":{"label":"Distributed tx (fintech)"}},"10":{"en":{"label":"System design + rate limit"},"vi":{"label":"System design + rate limit"}},"11":{"en":{"label":"System design cases"},"vi":{"label":"System design cases"}},"12":{"en":{"label":"Architecture & patterns"},"vi":{"label":"Architecture & patterns"}},"13":{"en":{"label":"Security & OAuth2"},"vi":{"label":"Security & OAuth2"}},"14":{"en":{"label":"DevOps · K8s · best practices"},"vi":{"label":"DevOps · K8s · best practices"}},"15":{"en":{"label":"Network & I/O models"},"vi":{"label":"Network & I/O models"}},"16":{"en":{"label":"Project · concurrency · whiteboard"},"vi":{"label":"Project · concurrency · whiteboard"}},"17":{"en":{"label":"REST API design"},"vi":{"label":"Thiết kế REST API"}},"18":{"en":{"label":"Query optimisation"},"vi":{"label":"Tối ưu query"}},"19":{"en":{"label":"DSA & LeetCode"},"vi":{"label":"DSA & LeetCode"}},"20":{"en":{"label":"Observability & SRE"},"vi":{"label":"Observability & SRE"}},"21":{"en":{"label":"Linux & production debug"},"vi":{"label":"Linux & production debug"}},"22":{"en":{"label":"Low-level design / OOD"},"vi":{"label":"Low-level design / OOD"}},"23":{"en":{"label":"Java concurrency coding"},"vi":{"label":"Java concurrency coding"}},"24":{"en":{"label":"Domain-Driven Design"},"vi":{"label":"Domain-Driven Design"}},"25":{"en":{"label":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track"},"vi":{"label":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track"}},"26":{"en":{"label":"Testing"},"vi":{"label":"Testing"}},"27":{"en":{"label":"API Gateway & Identity Edge"},"vi":{"label":"API Gateway & Identity Edge"}},"28":{"en":{"label":"Distributed Lock & Lease"},"vi":{"label":"Distributed Lock & Lease"}}},"reviewedAt":{"13-security-oauth2.oauth2-oidc.q2":"2026-10-25","13-security-oauth2.oauth2-oidc.q1":"2026-10-25","13-security-oauth2.jwt-tokens.q1":"2026-08-01","13-security-oauth2.jwt-tokens.q3":"2026-08-01","13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q2":"2026-08-01","13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q3":"2026-10-26","13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q4":"2026-10-26","13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q5":"2026-08-01","13-security-oauth2.secure-coding-at-the-boundary.q3":"2026-10-26","13-security-oauth2.secure-coding-at-the-boundary.q4":"2026-10-26","13-security-oauth2.jwt-tokens.q4":"2026-10-25","13-security-oauth2.jwt-tokens.q2":"2026-09-12","13-security-oauth2.protecting-the-data-you-store.q1":"2026-09-12","13-security-oauth2.protecting-the-data-you-store.q7":"2026-09-12","13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q1":"2026-09-12","13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q2":"2026-09-12","13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q3":"2026-09-12","13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q4":"2026-09-12","13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q5":"2026-09-12","13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q6":"2026-09-12"},"lang":"en","body":{"sections":[{"title":"OAuth2 & OIDC","items":[{"id":"13-security-oauth2.oauth2-oidc.q1","q":"OAuth 2.x: what are the roles, modern flows, and flows to avoid?","a":"**Roles:** the [[b:resource owner]] grants access; the [[b:client]] requests it; the [[b:authorization server]] authenticates/authorizes and issues tokens; the [[b:resource server]] enforces the token for its API.\n\nDeployments can combine these roles, but the protocol roles remain distinct. OAuth governs delegated [[g:authorization]]—it does not authenticate a user or define a login UI. OIDC is the authentication protocol layered on that OAuth authorization.\n\nChoose a flow from the client and user context:\n\n- **Authorization Code + PKCE** for browser, native, and other user-facing clients. A server-side BFF can keep tokens away from browser JavaScript; a pure SPA remains a public client.\n- **Client Credentials** when a confidential workload acts as itself, not as a user. Prefer workload identity, private-key authentication, or federation over a long-lived shared secret.\n- **Device Authorization Grant** for input-constrained devices when supported.\n- **Refresh Token Grant** renews an existing authorization; issuance is optional and requires replay protection.\n- Avoid **Implicit** and **Resource Owner Password Credentials**. RFC 9700 recommends authorization-code responses and removes password handling from clients.\n\n:::deep\nOAuth 2.1 is still an [[o:Internet-Draft (draft-16, September 2026)]], not an RFC. Use RFC 9700, the OAuth 2.0 Security BCP, as normative deployed guidance.\n\nFor browser public clients, RFC 10017 (published August 2026) is the browser-specific BCP and requires Authorization Code + PKCE. Provider support, metadata, redirect registration, and exact version/configuration remain deployment questions.\n\nScopes and audiences must be least-privilege and resource-specific. Client Credentials must not manufacture end-user identity; when service A calls B on behalf of a user, use an explicit delegation/token-exchange design with downscoped audience rather than blindly forwarding the original token.\n\nPasskeys/WebAuthn and MFA are authentication methods at the authorization server/identity provider, not OAuth grants. They strengthen how the user signs in while OAuth/OIDC governs tokens and delegation.\n:::\n\n:::deep\n**What would change this answer** — state these before defending the design; each is an assumption the interviewer can ask you to justify, and a measurement that would falsify it:\n\n- The browser architecture — direct SPA, BFF, server-rendered, native, or hybrid — because it decides token storage and PKCE guidance.\n- The IdP/Keycloak and Spring Security versions, signing algorithms, issuer topology and regions in scope.\n- The maximum revocation window for access and refresh tokens, and how emergency revocation works during an IdP or JWKS outage.\n- Whether service-to-service callers are workload identities, OAuth clients, user-delegated calls, or all three — and which domain actions need resource-level authorization beyond scopes.\n- What falsifies a JWT: key rotation that cannot meet the outage bound, revocation latency beyond the risk window, or a payload that must stay confidential.\n- What falsifies a policy cache: a stale policy permitting a prohibited write, a policy version that cannot be observed, or unproven recovery after a policy-store outage.\n:::","difficulty":"core"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.oauth2-oidc.q2","q":"How does Authorization Code + PKCE work, and why is PKCE needed?","a":"The client creates a high-entropy `code_verifier`, derives an S256 `code_challenge`, stores verifier plus transaction state, and redirects the browser to the authorization endpoint.\n\nAfter authentication/consent, the authorization server sends a short-lived, one-time code to an exactly registered redirect URI. The client redeems that code over the token endpoint and proves possession of the verifier.\n\n<figure class='dgm'><svg viewBox='0 0 680 318' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='IBM Plex Mono, monospace' font-size='10.5' text-anchor='middle'>\n<rect x='0' y='0' width='680' height='318' rx='8' fill='#FBFCFE'/>\n<rect x='20' y='14' width='110' height='34' rx='7' fill='#E7EAF6' stroke='#3D4E8F'/><text x='75' y='35' fill='#3D4E8F' font-weight='600'>Browser</text>\n<rect x='155' y='14' width='110' height='34' rx='7' fill='#E4F1EB' stroke='#0B6E4F'/><text x='210' y='35' fill='#085239' font-weight='600'>Client</text>\n<rect x='490' y='14' width='180' height='34' rx='7' fill='#FFF4D9' stroke='#C58A19'/><text x='580' y='35' fill='#76500C' font-weight='600'>Authorization server</text>\n<path d='M75 48V286M210 48V286M580 48V286' stroke='#B7C0CF' stroke-dasharray='3 4'/>\n<text x='222' y='80' fill='#14233A' text-anchor='start'>① keep verifier · challenge = S256(verifier)</text>\n<path d='M77 112H574' stroke='#3D4E8F' stroke-width='1.7' marker-end='url(#pk132b)'/><text x='326' y='105' fill='#3D4E8F'>② GET /authorize · code_challenge · state</text>\n<path d='M578 150H83' stroke='#3D4E8F' stroke-width='1.7' marker-end='url(#pk132b)'/><text x='326' y='143' fill='#3D4E8F'>③ 302 to exact redirect_uri · one-time code</text>\n<path d='M77 172H202' stroke='#3D4E8F' stroke-width='1.7' marker-end='url(#pk132b)'/><text x='140' y='190' fill='#3D4E8F'>code</text>\n<path d='M212 222H574' stroke='#0B6E4F' stroke-width='1.7' marker-end='url(#pk132g)'/><text x='393' y='215' fill='#0B6E4F'>④ POST /token · code + code_verifier</text>\n<text x='570' y='248' fill='#14233A' text-anchor='end'>⑤ S256(verifier) = challenge ?</text>\n<path d='M578 270H218' stroke='#0B6E4F' stroke-width='1.7' marker-end='url(#pk132g)'/><text x='398' y='263' fill='#0B6E4F'>tokens, or invalid_grant if not</text>\n<text x='340' y='306' fill='#14233A' font-weight='600'>A code stolen on the front channel is useless without the verifier</text>\n<defs><marker id='pk132b' markerWidth='9' markerHeight='9' refX='7' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 z' fill='#3D4E8F'/></marker><marker id='pk132g' markerWidth='9' markerHeight='9' refX='7' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 z' fill='#0B6E4F'/></marker></defs>\n</svg><figcaption>Blue arrows travel through the browser, where a code can leak; green ones are the client's back channel. Only the client holds the verifier, so only it can redeem the code.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"c\"># authorization request (line breaks only for readability)</span>\nGET /authorize?response_type=code&amp;client_id=web&amp;\n    code_challenge=BASE64URL(SHA256(verifier))&amp;code_challenge_method=S256&amp;\n    state=BOUND_TO_BROWSER_SESSION&amp;redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fapp.example%2Fcallback\n<span class=\"c\">    </span>\n<span class=\"c\"># back-channel token request: the stolen code is useless without verifier</span>\nPOST /token\ngrant_type=authorization_code&amp;code=ONE_TIME_CODE&amp;code_verifier=ORIGINAL_VERIFIER</code></pre>\n\nPKCE prevents authorization-code interception and injection. RFC 9700 requires PKCE protection for public clients; RFC 10017 (published August 2026) makes Authorization Code + PKCE the browser public-client baseline.\n\nConfidential clients should also use it as defense in depth. The current OAuth 2.1 draft also makes PKCE part of the default authorization-code flow, but OAuth 2.1 is [[r:not yet an RFC]].\n\nCheck the authorization server's metadata and provider/version compatibility before assuming every deployment exposes the same options.\n\n:::deep\nPKCE is one control in a transaction-bound flow:\n\n- use S256, high verifier entropy, one-time codes, short code lifetime, and exact redirect-URI matching;\n- bind `state` to the user-agent session for request/response correlation and CSRF defense; in OIDC also validate `nonce` in the ID token and the authorization-response issuer where applicable;\n- at token validation, check issuer and audience so a code/token from another authorization server or resource cannot be substituted;\n- do not put access tokens in redirect URLs.\n\n**What draft-16 (September 2026) tightened:** the `plain` challenge method is gone, leaving S256 or a secure extension method, and the authorization server must send `iss` in the authorization response (RFC 9207). The client still picks its mix-up defence, but `iss` is now always there to check.\n\nFor browser apps, prefer a BFF when the deployment can support one: the BFF is the confidential OAuth client, stores tokens server-side, and exposes a Secure/HttpOnly/SameSite session cookie with CSRF protection.\n\nA pure SPA uses Authorization Code + PKCE, minimizes token lifetime/storage, enforces a strong CSP, and assumes XSS can act with any token JavaScript can access.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.oauth2-oidc.q3","q":"What's the difference between OAuth2 and OIDC?","a":"<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>OAuth2</th><th>OIDC</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Answers</td><td>'what is the app allowed to do'</td><td>'who is the user'</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Token</td><td>Access Token</td><td>+ ID Token (JWT, `sub`, claims)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>authorization</td><td>authentication (+ authorization)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nOIDC is built on top of OAuth2, adding the ID Token + the `/userinfo` endpoint. Modern SSO uses OIDC.\n\n:::deep\n- OAuth2 returns an *access token* (what the app is allowed to do) — it does NOT standardize saying who the user is\n- OIDC adds the ID Token (JWT: `sub`, `iss`, `aud`, `email`, `auth_time`), `/userinfo`, discovery via `/.well-known/openid-configuration`, and JWKS\n\n[[r:Common mistake]]: using the *access token* to determine user identity (wrong — the access token is for the resource server); using the ID token to call an API (wrong — the ID token is for the client to know who the user is).\n\nOTA: OIDC for B2C login (via Google/social IdP), access tokens for calling internal services with the appropriate scope.\n:::","difficulty":"core"}]},{"title":"JWT & tokens","items":[{"id":"13-security-oauth2.jwt-tokens.q1","q":"What is the JWT structure, and how is it signed and verified?","a":"A commonly used signed JWT is the compact JWS form `header.payload.signature`, with each part base64url-encoded. The payload is [[r:not encrypted]] unless a separate JWE design is used.\n\n- Header: a pinned `alg`, a rotation key ID `kid`, and an explicit `typ` such as the access-token profile expected by the API.\n- Claims: `iss`, `sub`, `aud`, `exp`, `iat`, optionally `nbf`/`jti`, and authorization data such as scopes.\n- Signature: integrity/authenticity over the encoded header and payload using a trusted key.\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"c\">// Validation order; use a maintained JOSE/OAuth library, not home-grown crypto</span>\n<span class=\"f\">requireExpectedTypAndAllowedAlgorithm</span>(header);\nKey key = trustedJwks.<span class=\"f\">resolve</span>(header.kid());\nClaims c = jws.<span class=\"f\">verifyThenDecode</span>(key);\n<span class=\"f\">requireIssuer</span>(c, EXPECTED_ISSUER);\n<span class=\"f\">requireAudience</span>(c, THIS_API);\n<span class=\"f\">requireTimeWindow</span>(c.nbf(), c.exp(), ALLOWED_CLOCK_SKEW);\n<span class=\"f\">requireScopesAndSubjectPolicy</span>(c);</code></pre>\n\n:::warn Parsing is not validation\nDo not authorize from unverified claims. Reject `alg: none`, algorithm/key-type confusion, unexpected `typ`, untrusted `jku`/`x5u`, missing issuer/audience/time claims, and tokens intended for another API or token class. An ID token is not an API access token.\n:::\n\n:::deep\nJWKS handling is production logic: fetch only from issuer configuration you trust; cache keys; rate-limit refresh on an unknown `kid`; retain previous keys through the maximum token lifetime; and overlap old/new signing keys during rotation.\n\nA naïve “refetch on every unknown kid” path is a denial-of-service vector, while a cache that never refreshes breaks rotation.\n\n`typ`, issuer, audience, key set, and validation rules together prevent cross-JWT and token-substitution attacks. Signature validity says who signed bytes; authorization still has to check tenant, subject, scope/role, resource ownership, and current policy.\n\nKeep claims minimal, never put secrets in a readable payload, never log the token, and choose access-token lifetime from replay risk and revocation capability rather than a universal number of minutes.\n\n**Validation checklist:**\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Required decision</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Issuer and key source</td><td>trust one configured issuer; derive JWKS only from its trusted metadata</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Algorithm and signature</td><td>allow-list the algorithm/key family, resolve a trusted <code>kid</code>, then verify the signature</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Token profile</td><td>require the expected <code>typ</code> and token class; an ID token is not an API access token</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Issuer and audience</td><td>match <code>iss</code> exactly and require this API's expected <code>aud</code> or resource</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Time window</td><td>require <code>exp</code>, apply <code>nbf</code> when present, and bound clock skew explicitly</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Permission</td><td>require the scope/role for this operation; a valid signature is not permission</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Resource ownership</td><td>load tenant, owner, object state, and action policy from trusted server state</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nCryptographic checks can be delegated to a maintained library, but resource ownership and business authorization remain the service's responsibility.\n\nBound JWKS recovery with a single-flight refresh per issuer. An unknown <code>kid</code> may trigger one bounded metadata refresh; concurrent requests share it and repeated misses use backoff/negative caching.\n\nReject the token until the new key is fetched and verified. Never fetch an attacker-selected URL.\n:::","difficulty":"core"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.jwt-tokens.q2","q":"JWT (stateless) vs opaque token / session — what are the trade-offs?","a":"<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Choice</th><th>Good fit</th><th>Main cost or failure</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Short-lived JWT</td><td>local validation with a bounded revocation window</td><td>stale claims, key/audience errors, readable payload</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Opaque + introspection</td><td>central revocation or active-state checks</td><td>IdP latency/availability, cache staleness, load</td></tr>\n<tr><td>BFF + server session</td><td>sensitive browser session with a backend</td><td>session store, CSRF, cookie policy, BFF availability</td></tr>\n<tr><td>DPoP-bound token (RFC 9449)</td><td>public/browser/native client needs sender-constraining and provider support</td><td>key/nonce/replay handling; XSS can still act while running</td></tr>\n<tr><td>mTLS-bound token (RFC 8705)</td><td>workload/partner owns PKI and certificate-bound tokens</td><td>CA/cert rotation, proxy termination, device support</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n:::tip Bottom line\nJWT trades revocability for scale. Fintech typically uses: short-lived JWT access token + revocable refresh token + blacklist/rotation.\n:::\n\n:::deep\n- need immediate revocation (locking a fraudulent account) → short-lived access token + introspection for sensitive operations, or an opaque token\n- [[g:common hybrid pattern]]: JWT access token for 5–15 minutes (validated locally) + server-side refresh token *rotation* (revocable, reuse detection → revokes the whole family) + `jti` denylist for emergencies\n\n'JWT trades revocability for statelessness/scale; we compensate with a short TTL + revocation at the refresh layer' — [[r:avoid saying 'JWT is always better than sessions']].\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.jwt-tokens.q3","q":"Access token vs refresh token, and what's the revocation strategy?","a":"- An [[b:access token]] is presented to a resource server and should be audience-restricted, least-privilege, and short enough for the replay/revocation risk.\n- A [[b:refresh token]] is a higher-value credential used only at the authorization server. Issuance and lifetime are policy decisions; inactivity, consent, credential, device, or risk changes can terminate the grant early.\n\nFor public clients, RFC 9700 requires refresh tokens to be [[g:sender-constrained]] (for example DPoP) or rotated with reuse detection. Rotation issues a new token on every refresh, invalidates the predecessor, and revokes the active family when reuse reveals likely theft.\n\nConfidential clients must authenticate and the server must bind the token to that client.\n\nStorage follows the platform threat model: a BFF keeps OAuth tokens server-side and uses a Secure/HttpOnly/SameSite cookie plus CSRF defense; native apps use OS-protected storage; browser-only apps minimize token lifetime and commonly keep access tokens in memory.\n\nHttpOnly prevents JavaScript reads, not requests made by an XSS payload, so CSP and output safety still matter.\n\n**The two credentials side by side, because almost every mistake here is treating them alike:**\n\n<table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Access token</th><th>Refresh token</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Presented to</b></td><td>every resource server</td><td>the authorization server only, never an API</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Typical lifetime</b></td><td>5–15 minutes</td><td>hours to months, bounded by inactivity as well as absolute age</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Blast radius if stolen</b></td><td>bounded by the expiry you chose</td><td>a standing ability to mint access tokens</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Validation</b></td><td>local signature check, or introspection when central revocation matters more than latency</td><td>always a server-side lookup — it has state</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Revocation</b></td><td>usually just expiry; that is the trade a short lifetime pays for</td><td>immediate, and it takes the whole family with it</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Storage</b></td><td>memory in a browser app, OS keystore on native</td><td>never in a browser app without a BFF holding it server-side</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n**Rotation with reuse detection, in the order the checks must happen:**\n<pre><code><span class=\"c\">// 1. the refresh arrives; look it up by its family, not just by its value</span>\nFamily family = store.<span class=\"f\">byToken</span>(presented);\n<span class=\"k\">if</span> (family == <span class=\"k\">null</span>) <span class=\"k\">throw</span> <span class=\"k\">new</span> <span class=\"f\">InvalidGrant</span>();\n<span class=\"c\">//</span>\n<span class=\"c\">// 2. THE detection: this token was already spent. Only two things explain that —</span>\n<span class=\"c\">//    a stolen copy being replayed, or a client that retried. Both are handled the</span>\n<span class=\"c\">//    same way, because you cannot tell them apart and only one is safe to assume.</span>\n<span class=\"k\">if</span> (presented.<span class=\"f\">isSuperseded</span>()) {\n    store.<span class=\"f\">revokeFamily</span>(family.<span class=\"f\">id</span>());     <span class=\"r\">// kill every descendant, not just this one</span>\n    audit.<span class=\"f\">refreshReuse</span>(family.<span class=\"f\">subject</span>(), family.<span class=\"f\">id</span>());\n    <span class=\"k\">throw</span> <span class=\"k\">new</span> <span class=\"f\">InvalidGrant</span>();\n}\n<span class=\"c\">//</span>\n<span class=\"c\">// 3. only now issue the pair, and mark the predecessor spent in the same write</span>\n<span class=\"k\">return</span> store.<span class=\"f\">rotate</span>(family, presented);</code></pre>\n\n:::warn The retry that looks exactly like theft\nA client that loses the response to a refresh will retry with the old token and trip reuse detection — the user is logged out for a network blip. Absorb it at the client (one in-flight refresh per app, shared by every waiting request), not by weakening the check on the server.\n:::\n\n:::deep\nRevocation is layered:\n\n- revoke the authorization/refresh-token family on logout, consent withdrawal, account compromise, credential reset, or reuse detection;\n- use opaque access tokens with introspection when immediate central revocation outweighs the availability/latency cost, or a short-lived signed token when local validation is the better trade-off;\n- use a `jti` denylist only for bounded emergency cases with an explicit retention/availability design;\n- require reauthentication or step-up with WebAuthn/MFA for high-risk operations, even when a token is valid;\n- consider DPoP or mTLS sender-constrained access tokens where bearer-token replay is a material risk.\n\nNever log tokens or URLs/headers containing them. Redact at ingestion, restrict security-log access, and alert on refresh reuse without recording the credential itself (20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q4).\n:::","difficulty":"core"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.jwt-tokens.q4","difficulty":"advanced","q":"Where should a browser store JWTs: cookies, sessionStorage, or localStorage?","a":"**The default answer is none of those places for the JWT itself.** JWT is only a token format. Storage depends on whether it is an access, refresh or ID token and whether browser JavaScript must ever possess it.\n\nFor a first-party web application with a backend, prefer a [[g:Backend for Frontend (BFF)]]. The BFF keeps OAuth access and refresh tokens server-side. The browser receives an opaque, random session identifier in a hardened cookie—not the JWT.\n\n<figure class='dgm'><svg viewBox='0 0 680 244' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='IBM Plex Mono, monospace' font-size='10.5' text-anchor='middle'>\n<rect x='20' y='78' width='126' height='62' rx='8' fill='#E7EAF6' stroke='#3D4E8F'/><text x='83' y='103' fill='#3D4E8F' font-weight='600'>Browser</text><text x='83' y='122' fill='#42536C'>no OAuth token</text>\n<rect x='208' y='52' width='184' height='114' rx='8' fill='#E4F1EB' stroke='#0B6E4F'/><text x='300' y='80' fill='#085239' font-weight='600'>BFF</text><text x='300' y='102' fill='#42536C'>server-side session</text><text x='300' y='121' fill='#42536C'>access + refresh token</text><text x='300' y='140' fill='#42536C'>CSRF enforcement</text>\n<rect x='454' y='26' width='204' height='62' rx='8' fill='#FFF4D9' stroke='#C58A19'/><text x='556' y='51' fill='#76500C' font-weight='600'>Authorization server</text><text x='556' y='70' fill='#42536C'>code → tokens</text>\n<rect x='454' y='132' width='204' height='62' rx='8' fill='#F1EAF7' stroke='#7C5AA6'/><text x='556' y='157' fill='#5E3E80' font-weight='600'>Resource API</text><text x='556' y='176' fill='#42536C'>Bearer access token</text>\n<path d='M148 96H204' stroke='#42536C' stroke-width='1.7' marker-end='url(#jt134a)'/><path d='M206 124H150' stroke='#42536C' stroke-width='1.7' marker-end='url(#jt134a)'/><text x='177' y='83' fill='#42536C'>request + cookie</text><text x='177' y='151' fill='#42536C'>response</text>\n<path d='M394 83L450 64' stroke='#C58A19' stroke-width='1.7' marker-end='url(#jt134y)'/><path d='M394 132L450 154' stroke='#7C5AA6' stroke-width='1.7' marker-end='url(#jt134p)'/>\n<text x='340' y='222' fill='#14233A' font-weight='600'>Cookie is a session handle · OAuth tokens stay outside JavaScript</text>\n<defs><marker id='jt134a' markerWidth='9' markerHeight='9' refX='7' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 z' fill='#42536C'/></marker><marker id='jt134y' markerWidth='9' markerHeight='9' refX='7' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 z' fill='#C58A19'/></marker><marker id='jt134p' markerWidth='9' markerHeight='9' refX='7' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 z' fill='#7C5AA6'/></marker></defs>\n</svg><figcaption>The BFF is the OAuth client. It adds state and availability work in exchange for keeping bearer tokens out of the page.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Place</th><th>XSS consequence</th><th>CSRF consequence</th><th>Lifetime</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>BFF + HttpOnly cookie</b></td><td>script cannot read the cookie <br>but can make authenticated same-origin calls</td><td>browser sends cookie automatically <br>needs CSRF defense</td><td>cookie + server session policy</td><td><b>preferred</b> when a backend is available</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Memory</b></td><td>running malicious script can request or use a token <br>persistent theft is harder</td><td>Authorization header is not automatic</td><td>lost on reload/tab close</td><td>best direct-SPA baseline</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>sessionStorage</b></td><td>same-origin script can read and exfiltrate it</td><td>not sent automatically</td><td>one tab; survives reload</td><td>shorter exposure, not XSS-safe</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>localStorage</b></td><td>same-origin script can read every persisted token</td><td>not sent automatically</td><td>persists across restarts and tabs</td><td>avoid for credentials</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>JS-readable cookie</b></td><td>readable like Web Storage</td><td>also sent automatically to matching requests</td><td>cookie expiry</td><td>worst properties combined; do not use</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n**A hardened session cookie looks like this:**\n\n<pre><code>Set-Cookie: __Host-session=RANDOM_OPAQUE_ID; Path=/; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax\nCache-Control: no-store</code></pre>\n\n- <code>HttpOnly</code> prevents JavaScript from reading the cookie; it does not stop XSS from operating the application through the victim's browser.\n- <code>Secure</code> restricts transport to HTTPS. The <code>__Host-</code> prefix also requires <code>Path=/</code> and no <code>Domain</code>, preventing broad subdomain scope.\n- Choose <code>SameSite=Strict</code> or <code>Lax</code> from the navigation flow. SameSite is defense in depth, not a universal replacement for a CSRF token, Origin/Fetch-Metadata checks and correct non-mutating GET semantics.\n- Rotate the session identifier on login and privilege change; enforce idle and absolute expiry server-side. Logout deletes server session state, clears the cookie and revokes the refresh-token family where supported.\n\n**What if the product is a pure SPA that calls an API directly?** Use Authorization Code + PKCE. Keep a short-lived, narrowly scoped access token in memory or an isolated service worker. Send it in <code>Authorization: Bearer</code>.\n\nAccept that reload loses it and re-establish authorization through the IdP session.\n\nA service worker can reduce direct token exfiltration only if it never returns tokens to the page or persists them in storage shared with the window.\n\nAn XSS payload can still ask that trusted component to make allowed calls while it is running; isolation is not an XSS cure.\n\nIf the authorization server issues refresh tokens to a public browser client, use rotation or sender-constrained tokens when supported, detect reuse, cap absolute lifetime and avoid persistent JavaScript-readable storage.\n\nA stolen bearer token remains usable by whoever holds it until expiry or revocation policy intervenes (13-security-oauth2.jwt-tokens.q3).\n\n**Token type matters:**\n\n- **Access token:** API credential. Keep it short-lived and out of URLs, logs, analytics and crash reports.\n- **Refresh token:** long-lived authority to mint access tokens. Keep it server-side whenever possible; it deserves stronger protection than the access token.\n- **ID token:** a client authentication result, not an API credential. Validate it during the OIDC flow, derive the local session and do not persist it merely to display profile fields.\n\n:::warn Encryption in localStorage does not close the boundary\nIf JavaScript can decrypt the token, injected JavaScript can call the same decrypt path or use the token through your code. Moving the encryption key into the same origin only rearranges the secret.\n:::\n\n:::tip Decision rule\nHave a backend? Use BFF plus an opaque HttpOnly cookie. Pure SPA? Prefer memory/service-worker isolation and short-lived access tokens. sessionStorage is a bounded compromise; localStorage is for non-sensitive UI state, not bearer credentials.\n:::\n\n:::deep\n- Cookie size and transport matter: a JWT in a cookie may approach browser limits and is attached to every matching request. An opaque session id is smaller and gives logout/revocation one server-side control point.\n- Cookie authentication changes the API threat model from header-bearer handling to ambient authority. CORS does not replace CSRF protection; CORS governs response access, while a forged request can still have effects.\n- CSP, Trusted Types, dependency control and output encoding reduce XSS likelihood. None converts JavaScript-readable storage into a protected credential vault.\n- Do not copy this browser decision to native mobile. Native apps use the operating system's protected credential storage and an external user-agent flow (RFC 8252).\n\nThe normative browser-specific source for this decision is RFC 10017, *OAuth 2.0 for Browser-Based Applications*, published in August 2026.\n\nRFC 9700 remains the general OAuth 2.0 Security BCP. Re-check provider support and the target client architecture before applying either document as a deployment recipe.\n:::"}]},{"title":"Designing the auth system","items":[{"id":"13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q1","q":"How would you design the authentication/authorization system for microservices (SSO + gateway)?","a":"Design three identities separately: the human/session, the OAuth client, and the calling workload.\n\n- A central [[b:authorization server/IdP]] handles OIDC login, WebAuthn/passkeys/MFA, consent, token issuance, keys, and session risk.\n- A [[b:gateway or BFF]] validates the external protocol at the edge, strips any client-supplied identity headers, and creates trusted internal context. A BFF keeps browser tokens server-side.\n- Each [[b:resource-owning service]] enforces scopes plus domain authorization such as tenant, object ownership, amount, and state transition. Edge validation alone cannot decide those invariants.\n- Workloads authenticate with short-lived federated identity, mTLS/SPIFFE, or an OAuth confidential-client method; avoid one shared static credential across services.\n\n:::tip Choose one internal token contract\nEither services validate an externally issued token with the exact issuer/audience profile, or a trusted edge exchanges it for a short-lived, downscoped internal token.\n:::\n\nDo not forward arbitrary identity headers or every original bearer token through all hops. Preserve the actor/delegation chain explicitly when a service acts for a user.\n:::deep\nValidation and authorization belong at different boundaries. The gateway can reject malformed/expired tokens, enforce coarse scopes, rate-limit, and translate protocols. The ledger still verifies that this subject may move money from this account and requires step-up for a high-risk action.\n\nFor local JWT validation, distribute trust through OIDC discovery/JWKS with bounded caches and rotation overlap. For opaque tokens, design introspection availability, caching, and revocation semantics.\n\nInternal calls need workload identity even when user context is present; one credential answers “which service?”, the delegated token answers “for which actor and authority?”\n\nPropagate an absolute deadline, trace context, and only the minimum identity attributes. Log authorization decisions and policy version without logging credentials or sensitive claims.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q2","q":"What is mTLS, and how is it used for service-to-service authentication?","a":"[[g:mTLS]] authenticates both TLS peers with certificates while encrypting the channel. It proves possession of a private key bound to an accepted certificate/trust domain; it does not by itself decide whether that workload may perform a business action.\n\nCommon uses:\n\n- workload-to-workload identity with certificates/SVIDs rotated by a mesh, workload-identity platform, or PKI automation;\n- OAuth client authentication and certificate-bound access tokens under RFC 8705, reducing replay from a different TLS client;\n- B2B partner APIs where both organizations can operate certificate issuance, renewal, revocation, and incident procedures.\n\nDPoP (RFC 9449) is an application-layer proof-of-possession option that can sender-constrain tokens without requiring the same TLS-client-certificate topology. Neither mechanism protects a fully compromised client that loses both token and key.\n\n:::deep\nA service mesh can automate issuance, rotation, and transport policy, but it is not “security with no code or operations.”\n\nDefine trust domains, namespace/service authorization, certificate lifetime, clock-skew behavior, CA/root rotation, revocation/compromise response, telemetry, and fail-open/fail-closed behavior.\n\nTest that plaintext bypass paths are closed.\n\nUse mTLS where the threat model and platform justify it; “every request must use a mesh” is not a universal architecture rule. It adds proxies or library integration, control-plane dependencies, latency/resource cost, and another policy layer.\n\nCloud workload federation or signed workload tokens may fit some boundaries better.\n\nKeep service identity and end-user delegation separate. An mTLS peer may be `booking-service`, while a scoped token says it is acting for user U on booking B. Authorization should evaluate both when both matter.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q3","q":"How should OWASP Top 10:2025 and API Security Top 10:2023 shape backend controls?","a":"Treat both lists as [[o:awareness and threat-modelling inputs]], not a compliance checklist.\n\nOWASP Top 10:2025 emphasizes broken access control, security misconfiguration, software supply-chain failures, cryptographic/injection/insecure-design/authentication failures, integrity, logging/alerting, and mishandled exceptional conditions.\n\nAPI Security Top 10:2023 adds API-specific focus such as object/function/property authorization, resource consumption, sensitive business flows, SSRF, inventory, and unsafe consumption of upstream APIs.\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Risk</th><th>Engineering controls</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>injection / XSS / CSRF</td><td>parameter binding; context-aware output encoding and CSP; SameSite plus CSRF tokens for cookie-authenticated writes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>BOLA/IDOR and function/property authorization</td><td>server-side tenant, object, field, and action checks on every path; deny by default; test negative cases</td></tr>\n<tr><td>resource and sensitive-flow abuse</td><td>payload/page limits, deadlines, quotas, bot/fraud controls, idempotency, and cost-aware admission</td></tr>\n<tr><td>SSRF / unsafe upstream APIs</td><td>destination allowlists, safe URL parsing, egress controls, response validation, timeouts,<br>and no blind redirect/credential forwarding</td></tr>\n<tr><td>supply chain and integrity</td><td>locked dependencies, SCA, SBOM, provenance, signed artifacts, isolated builds, and<br>signature/policy verification at deploy</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n:::deep\nMass assignment is prevented by explicit input DTOs/allowlists, not by hiding fields in the UI.\n\nError paths need the same authorization and data-minimization rules as success paths: fail closed where correctness/security requires it, return stable problem details, and avoid stack traces or secrets in responses.\n\nBuild a security test matrix from assets and abuse cases: cross-tenant IDs, role downgrade, hidden fields, large/deep payloads, race/replay, webhook SSRF, partner schema surprises, dependency compromise, key rotation, and unavailable policy/identity services.\n\nRun SAST/SCA/secret scanning early, DAST and authorization tests against a deployed system, then verify audit/alert signals through incident exercises.\n\nWAF and rate limiting are defense in depth, not substitutes for resource-level authorization, safe code, or business-flow anti-automation.\n\n**Negative test corpus:**\n\n- JWT: algorithm confusion/`alg: none`, wrong issuer or audience, invalid signature, unexpected <code>typ</code>, expired/early <code>nbf</code>, and unknown <code>kid</code> during rotation;\n- flow and session: replayed authorization code, <code>state</code>, OIDC <code>nonce</code>, or PKCE verifier; refresh-token reuse; and replayed DPoP proof;\n- boundary: mass assignment of <code>role</code>/<code>tenantId</code>, SSRF redirect/DNS-rebinding/metadata access, unsafe polymorphic deserialization/XXE/deep payload, and cross-tenant object or function access.\n\nEach test should assert deny, no protected side effect, no secret in response/log, and a privacy-safe audit reason.\n:::","difficulty":"core"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q4","q":"How do you manage secrets properly?","a":"Prefer [[g:no long-lived secret]] when workload identity or short-lived federation can provide the credential. When a secret is necessary, keep it out of source, build logs, images, CI variables with broad scope, and client-visible configuration.\n\n- store and issue it through a managed secret/KMS/HSM system with least-privilege identity and audited access;\n- inject at runtime using the platform channel whose leakage risks you understand (agent/file/volume/API; environment variables can appear in dumps or diagnostics);\n- separate environments and tenants, limit blast radius, and use short-lived dynamic credentials where supported;\n- rotate on compromise, personnel/ownership change, cryptoperiod, or policy trigger; automate renewal and test overlap/rollback rather than rotating blindly and causing an outage;\n- scan current code, Git history, artifacts, logs, tickets, and images; a discovered credential is revoked/reissued, not merely deleted from the latest commit.\n\n:::deep\nA Kubernetes `Secret` is base64-encoded data, not encryption by itself. Configure etcd encryption at rest, tight RBAC, namespace/service-account isolation, audit, and preferably an external secret/KMS integration for high-value material.\n\nJWT signing-key rotation requires publishing old and new public keys under distinct `kid` values, switching the signer, retaining the old verification key through maximum token lifetime plus skew, then retiring it.\n\nRoot/CA, database, webhook, PSP/GDS, and encryption-key rotations need similarly explicit dependency order and rollback.\n\nUse envelope encryption for stored data: a data-encryption key encrypts data and a KMS/HSM-managed key encrypts the DEK. Maintain an inventory of secret owner, consumers, scope, creation/expiry, rotation method, and emergency revocation.\n\nNever log the material; log the secret identifier/version and access decision.\n:::","difficulty":"core"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q5","q":"How do you protect auth endpoints from brute-force/credential stuffing?","a":"- rate limit by IP + by account\n- exponential backoff/temporary lockout; CAPTCHA after N failed attempts\n- detect anomalies (login from an unusual location)\n- store passwords with a slow, salted hash function ([[g:bcrypt/argon2/scrypt]]), [[r:never plain MD5/SHA]]\n- MFA for sensitive operations\n- [[o:don't reveal 'whether the user exists']] through error messages\n\n:::deep\n- rate limit + lockout by IP *and* by account (defends against credential stuffing across many IPs against one account, &amp; brute-force against one account from one IP); exponential backoff; CAPTCHA past a threshold; anomaly detection (unfamiliar location/device → step-up MFA)\n- [[g:argon2id]] (recommended) / bcrypt / scrypt — NEVER plain MD5/SHA (fast, easy to brute-force on GPUs); don't leak 'user exists' via errors/timing (timing-safe)\n\nFintech/OTA: require phishing-resistant or step-up MFA where transaction risk, regulation and recovery policy demand it; prefer passkeys/WebAuthn where supported, and treat device/location signals as risk inputs rather than identity proof.\n\n[[r:Pitfall]]: lockout too aggressive → DoS against the real user (an attacker locks out the victim's account) → use CAPTCHA instead of a hard lockout, or lock by IP+account combined.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q6","q":"RBAC vs ABAC — which authorization model should you choose?","a":"<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>RBAC</th><th>ABAC</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Basis</td><td>roles (admin, teller)</td><td>attributes (user, resource, context, time)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pros</td><td>simple, easy to manage/audit</td><td>flexible for complex rules</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cons</td><td>role explosion as rules get complex</td><td>harder to debug/audit</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nMany systems combine both: RBAC as the base + ABAC for fine-grained rules.\n\n:::deep\n- ABAC example: 'an agent may only approve bookings below their branch's limit during working hours'\n- engines: OPA/Rego, Cedar for policy-as-code decoupled from business logic\n\nOTA: RBAC for customer/agent/admin; ABAC for 'agent from airline X only sees airline X's bookings', 'refunds above a threshold need higher-level approval'. [[o:Check permissions server-side]] (gateway + service), never trust the client; log every sensitive authorization decision (audit).\n:::","difficulty":"extra"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q7","q":"API key vs OAuth token — when do you use which?","a":"<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>API key</th><th>OAuth token</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Identifies</td><td>an application/partner</td><td>a user/scope</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Granularity</td><td>low</td><td>high (scope)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lifetime</td><td>static, long-lived</td><td>short-lived, revocable</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Good for</td><td>partner server-to-server</td><td>acting on behalf of a user</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n:::deep\n- [[b:API key]]: a secret string identifying an application/partner (not a user), used alongside an IP allowlist + rate limit/quota. [[r:Risk]]: static, long-lived, once leaked it stays usable indefinitely → needs rotation + a restricted scope\n- [[b:OAuth token]]: authorization scoped to a user/scope, short-lived, revocable\n\nOTA/fintech: B2C → OAuth/OIDC; internal services → mTLS + client-credentials; B2B partners → API key + mTLS + quota, or client-credentials with scope.\n\nManaging API keys: [[g:hash them at rest]] (like a password), use a prefix for identification, rotate without downtime (two keys active simultaneously during rotation), audit. [[r:Never embed an API key in a client/mobile app]] (too easy to extract).\n:::","difficulty":"extra"}]},{"title":"Keycloak in practice","items":[{"id":"13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q1","difficulty":"core","q":"What is Keycloak, and what are realms, clients and its core endpoints?","a":"Keycloak is an [[g:open-source identity provider]] (CNCF incubating since 2023) implementing OAuth 2.x, OIDC and SAML 2.0. It plays the [[o:authorization server]] role from (13-security-oauth2.oauth2-oidc.q1) so you do not build login, MFA, federation and token issuance yourself.\n\n**The object model, from the outside in:**\n\n<table class='cmp'><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>What it is</th><th>Gotcha</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>realm</code></td><td>An isolated tenant: its own users, roles, clients and [[o:signing keys]]</td><td>Cross-realm token trust does not exist. <code>master</code> is for administering Keycloak — never put application users there</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>client</code></td><td>One application asking for tokens</td><td><code>public</code> (SPA/mobile, no secret, PKCE required) vs <code>confidential</code> (backend, has a secret)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>client scope</code></td><td>Reusable bundle of claims/roles added to a token</td><td>Default scopes apply silently; <code>optional</code> ones only when requested</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>realm role</code> / <code>client role</code></td><td>Realm-wide vs per-client authority</td><td>They land in *different* token claims — see (13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q3)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>identity provider</code></td><td>Upstream federation (Google, Azure AD, LDAP)</td><td>Keycloak still mints its own token; the upstream one is not what your API sees</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n**Every endpoint is advertised by discovery** — never hardcode paths:\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"c\">// one document tells you every other URL</span>\nGET /realms/{realm}/.well-known/openid-configuration\n<span class=\"c\">//</span>\nauthorization_endpoint  /realms/{r}/protocol/openid-connect/auth\ntoken_endpoint          /realms/{r}/protocol/openid-connect/token\njwks_uri                /realms/{r}/protocol/openid-connect/certs\nend_session_endpoint    /realms/{r}/protocol/openid-connect/logout\nintrospection_endpoint  /realms/{r}/protocol/openid-connect/token/introspect</code></pre>\n\n:::tip Interview framing\nSay what Keycloak *replaces*: it is the authorization server, so your services keep only token *validation* — the resource-server half. That split is the whole reason to adopt it.\n:::\n\n:::deep\n- **`issuer` must match exactly**, character for character, including scheme, port and any trailing path. A Keycloak behind a reverse proxy that rewrites the host mints tokens whose `iss` your service then rejects — fix it with `KC_HOSTNAME` plus `--proxy-headers=xforwarded`, not by loosening validation.\n- Keycloak is stateful: it needs a real database ([[r:not the dev-mode H2]]) and, for a cluster, an Infinispan cache for sessions. Run `start` and not `start-dev` in production — the latter disables HTTPS requirements and uses an in-memory store.\n- The legacy `keycloak-spring-boot-starter` and its adapters are [[r:end-of-life]]. New services must use plain Spring Security OAuth2 resource server (13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q2); there is nothing Keycloak-specific left on the resource-server side.\n- Realm config belongs in version control. Export it and import declaratively (`kc.sh import`, or the Keycloak Operator's `KeycloakRealmImport`) so the IdP is reproducible rather than a hand-clicked snowflake.\n\n**Provider/version boundary.** The endpoint paths, realm behavior, and `KC_*` settings above are Keycloak examples, not OAuth/OIDC requirements. Discovery is the portable contract; verify the exact Keycloak distribution/version and reverse-proxy mode before copying an endpoint or configuration.\n:::"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q2","difficulty":"advanced","q":"How does a Spring Boot service validate a Keycloak token, and what must you verify?","a":"Your service is a [[g:resource server]]: it never talks to Keycloak per request. It fetches the [[o:JWKS]] once, caches the public keys, and verifies signatures locally — the mechanism from (13-security-oauth2.jwt-tokens.q1).\n\n**The whole configuration is one property:**\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"c\"># issuer-uri alone is enough — Spring reads discovery and finds the JWKS</span>\nspring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.issuer-uri: \\\n  https://kc.example.com/realms/fintech\n<span class=\"c\">#</span>\n<span class=\"c\"># it validates: signature, exp, nbf, and iss — but NOT audience</span></code></pre>\n\n**`aud` is not checked by default, and that is the trap.** Any token from the same realm — including one minted for a completely different client — verifies fine. Add the check explicitly:\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"k\">@Bean</span>\nJwtDecoder <span class=\"f\">jwtDecoder</span>(OAuth2ResourceServerProperties p) {\n    String issuer = p.getJwt().getIssuerUri();\n    NimbusJwtDecoder decoder = JwtDecoders.<span class=\"f\">fromIssuerLocation</span>(issuer);\n    decoder.<span class=\"f\">setJwtValidator</span>(<span class=\"k\">new</span> DelegatingOAuth2TokenValidator&lt;&gt;(\n        JwtValidators.<span class=\"f\">createDefaultWithIssuer</span>(issuer),      <span class=\"c\">// sig, exp, iss</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">new</span> JwtClaimValidator&lt;List&lt;String&gt;&gt;(               <span class=\"c\">// the missing one</span>\n            <span class=\"s\">\"aud\"</span>, aud -&gt; aud != <span class=\"k\">null</span> &amp;&amp; aud.<span class=\"f\">contains</span>(<span class=\"s\">\"orders-api\"</span>))));\n    <span class=\"k\">return</span> decoder;\n}</code></pre>\n\nGetting `aud` into the token at all takes a Keycloak-side [[o:audience mapper]] on the client — by default Keycloak puts the client only in `azp`, so `aud` may be absent entirely.\n\n:::warn Never trust these\nDo not authorize on `preferred_username` or `email` — both are mutable and `email` need not be verified. Bind rows to the [[r:immutable `sub`]], exactly as the local app does with the Google token (see the security model in `CLAUDE.md`).\n:::\n\n:::deep\n- **JWKS caching and key rotation.** Spring refreshes the key set when it sees an unknown `kid`, with a rate limit (default 5 minutes) so a bad token cannot turn into a JWKS stampede. Rotate keys in Keycloak *before* retiring the old one — it keeps serving both so in-flight tokens stay valid.\n- **Local verification means revocation is not immediate** — the same trade-off as (13-security-oauth2.jwt-tokens.q2).\n  - Usual answer: short access-token lifetimes (1–5 min) plus refresh-token rotation.\n  - `introspect` gives instant revocation, at the cost of a network hop and a hard Keycloak dependency on every request.\n- **`realm_access` is not a Spring authority.** Spring reads `scope`/`scp` by default, so `hasRole('ADMIN')` fails silently until you supply a converter (13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q3).\n- Keycloak must be reachable at startup only if you build the decoder eagerly; `JwtDecoders.fromIssuerLocation` fetches discovery immediately, which makes a cold Keycloak a boot failure. Use `NimbusJwtDecoder.withJwkSetUri(...)` for lazy fetching when boot ordering matters.\n\nThe Spring snippets describe one Spring Security configuration, not a timeless default. Defaults and extension APIs are version-sensitive.\n\nMake issuer discovery/JWKS and the audience validator explicit, pin the target Spring Security line, and test the exact dependency instead of assuming `issuer-uri` validates every claim.\n:::"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q3","difficulty":"advanced","q":"Where do Keycloak roles land in the token, and how do you map them to Spring authorities?","a":"Keycloak writes roles into [[o:nested claims]] that Spring Security does not read, so a fresh integration authenticates correctly and then fails every authorization check.\n\n**What is actually in the token:**\n\n<pre><code>{\n  <span class=\"s\">\"sub\"</span>: <span class=\"s\">\"8f14e45f-…\"</span>,           <span class=\"c\">// the only stable user id</span>\n  <span class=\"s\">\"azp\"</span>: <span class=\"s\">\"orders-web\"</span>,           <span class=\"c\">// which client asked</span>\n  <span class=\"s\">\"realm_access\"</span>: { <span class=\"s\">\"roles\"</span>: [<span class=\"s\">\"admin\"</span>, <span class=\"s\">\"teller\"</span>] },\n  <span class=\"s\">\"resource_access\"</span>: {              <span class=\"c\">// per-client roles</span>\n    <span class=\"s\">\"orders-api\"</span>: { <span class=\"s\">\"roles\"</span>: [<span class=\"s\">\"orders:write\"</span>] }\n  },\n  <span class=\"s\">\"scope\"</span>: <span class=\"s\">\"openid profile email\"</span>   <span class=\"c\">// &lt;-- Spring reads ONLY this</span>\n}</code></pre>\n\n**The converter that bridges the gap:**\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"k\">static</span> Collection&lt;GrantedAuthority&gt; <span class=\"f\">authorities</span>(Jwt jwt) {\n    Map&lt;String,Object&gt; realm = jwt.<span class=\"f\">getClaim</span>(<span class=\"s\">\"realm_access\"</span>);\n    <span class=\"k\">if</span> (realm == <span class=\"k\">null</span>) <span class=\"k\">return</span> List.<span class=\"f\">of</span>();\n    <span class=\"k\">var</span> roles = (Collection&lt;String&gt;) realm.<span class=\"f\">getOrDefault</span>(<span class=\"s\">\"roles\"</span>, List.<span class=\"f\">of</span>());\n    <span class=\"k\">return</span> roles.<span class=\"f\">stream</span>()\n        <span class=\"c\">// hasRole('ADMIN') looks for the ROLE_ prefix; omit it and</span>\n        <span class=\"c\">// only hasAuthority('admin') works — a classic silent 403</span>\n        .&lt;GrantedAuthority&gt;<span class=\"f\">map</span>(r -&gt; <span class=\"k\">new</span> SimpleGrantedAuthority(<span class=\"s\">\"ROLE_\"</span> + r.<span class=\"f\">toUpperCase</span>()))\n        .<span class=\"f\">toList</span>();\n}</code></pre>\n\n<table class='cmp'><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Authority spanning every service</td><td><code>realm_access</code> — a realm role</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Permission meaningful to one API only</td><td><code>resource_access</code> — a client role, so <code>orders:write</code> cannot leak authority into the payments service</td></tr>\n<tr><td>What the *client app* may ask for, not what the user is</td><td><code>scope</code> — delegation, not identity</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n:::deep\n- **Composite roles** expand at token-mint time, so `manager` containing `teller` arrives as both. Convenient, and the reason tokens quietly grow — a user in many groups can push the JWT past an 8 KB proxy header limit. Keep coarse roles in the token and resolve fine-grained permissions service-side.\n- **Group membership is not in the token by default.** Add a group-membership mapper if you need it, but prefer mapping groups to roles: groups are an organisational structure, roles are the authorization contract.\n- **`scope` vs role is a real interview distinction:** [[g:scope is what the client may request]], [[o:role is what the user is]].\n  - `scope=openid` with `realm_access.roles=[admin]` = an admin using an app granted only identity access.\n  - Both limits apply, and the effective permission is the intersection.\n- Prefer `@PreAuthorize` on the method over URL-pattern matching for anything non-trivial; the model is RBAC/ABAC from (13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q6), and Keycloak only supplies the attributes.\n\nRealm roles, client roles, claim names, role mappers, and Spring authority conversion here are Keycloak/provider configuration examples, not portable OAuth/OIDC claims. Pin the provider/version and test token fixtures after every mapper or authority-prefix change.\n:::"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q4","difficulty":"advanced","q":"How do services call each other with Keycloak, and what is token exchange for?","a":"Two different problems, two different grants — conflating them is a common design error.\n\n**Service acting as itself → `client_credentials`.** There is no user; the token's `sub` is the service account:\n\n<pre><code>POST /realms/fintech/protocol/openid-connect/token\nContent-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded\n<span class=\"c\">//</span>\ngrant_type=client_credentials\n&amp;client_id=batch-poster&amp;client_secret=…</code></pre>\n\n**Service acting on behalf of a user → [[o:token exchange]]** (RFC 8693). The gateway holds the user's token; the downstream service needs one scoped to itself but still carrying the user's identity:\n\n<pre><code>grant_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:token-exchange\n&amp;subject_token=&lt;the caller's access token&gt;\n&amp;audience=ledger-api          <span class=\"c\">// mint for this service</span>\n<span class=\"c\">// result: a NEW token, aud=ledger-api, sub still the original user</span></code></pre>\n\n<table class='cmp'><thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>When it is right</th><th>Cost</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Forward the same token</td><td>Trusted internal mesh, one audience</td><td>[[r:Any hop can replay it anywhere]] — audience is meaningless</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Token exchange</td><td>Crossing a trust boundary while keeping user identity</td><td>A Keycloak round-trip per hop (cache it for the token's lifetime)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>client_credentials</code></td><td>Batch jobs, cron, no user involved</td><td>Loses user identity — audit logs show only the service</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n:::warn Do not forward a user token indefinitely\nA token minted for `orders-api` and forwarded through four services can be replayed by any of them against any service in the realm. Set a real `audience` and validate it (13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q2) — that is what makes the boundary mean something.\n:::\n\n:::deep\n- **Token exchange is opt-in per client**, gated by fine-grained admin permissions: the exchanging client needs explicit authority over the target audience.\n  - It has moved between \"preview\" and supported across versions — [[o:check the feature flags for the version you actually run]] rather than assuming.\n- For pure machine-to-machine inside a cluster, [[g:mTLS]] (13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q2) may replace tokens entirely: the identity is the certificate and there is nothing to steal or replay. Use tokens when you need *user* identity to travel; use mTLS when you need *service* identity.\n- Bind the token to the client that holds it where the stakes justify it: mTLS-bound tokens (RFC 8705) or DPoP make a stolen bearer token useless elsewhere.\n- Cache service tokens and refresh before `exp`, honouring `expires_in`. A service that requests a fresh token per outbound call turns Keycloak into the bottleneck for the entire system.\n\nRFC 8693 defines a token-exchange framework, but this endpoint, grant enablement, permission model, and resulting claims are Keycloak/provider-specific. The example is not a portable guarantee: check the exact Keycloak version, feature flag, client policy, and target audience before depending on it.\n:::"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q5","difficulty":"extra","q":"What breaks when you run Keycloak in production?","a":"Keycloak is not a stateless sidecar — it is a [[r:database-backed, clustered, single point of failure for every login]]. The failure modes are operational, not conceptual.\n\n<table class='cmp'><thead><tr><th>Failure</th><th>Cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>invalid_token</code>, issuer mismatch</td><td>Proxy rewrites the host; <code>iss</code> no longer matches what services expect</td><td><code>KC_HOSTNAME</code> + <code>--proxy-headers=xforwarded</code>; never \"fix\" it by skipping validation</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Random logouts in a cluster</td><td>Sessions live in Infinispan; a node dies with the only copy</td><td>Distributed cache with <code>owners</code> &gt; 1, sticky sessions at the LB</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Slow logins under load</td><td>Every login writes to the DB; connection pool exhausted</td><td>Size the pool, watch DB latency — Keycloak is as fast as its database</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Token verification stalls everywhere</td><td>Keycloak down and services fetch JWKS per request</td><td>Cache the JWKS (default in Spring); verification must survive a Keycloak outage</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Upgrade breaks the realm</td><td>DB schema migrates in place, and it is one-way</td><td>Back up the DB, test the upgrade on a copy, pin the image tag</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n**The thing to say out loud:** if Keycloak is down, [[g:existing tokens keep working]] (local verification) but [[r:nobody can log in or refresh]]. That asymmetry decides your token lifetimes — long enough to ride out a restart, short enough that revocation means something.\n\n:::tip Capacity reality\nLogin is expensive (password hashing is deliberately slow — Argon2 by default in recent versions), token *verification* is free because it happens in your service. Size Keycloak for the login and refresh rate, not for request throughput.\n:::\n\n:::deep\n- **Run it HA, and treat the DB as the tier that matters:** at least two replicas, a real Postgres/MySQL with backups, PodDisruptionBudget, and readiness on `/health/ready` (management port 9000 in recent versions).\n  - The Keycloak Operator handles most of this on Kubernetes (14-devops-k8s-best-practices.docker-kubernetes.q6).\n- **Blast radius.** One realm for every application means one bad realm export takes down every login. Separate tenants into separate realms; keep `master` for administration only.\n- **Observability** — alert on login failure rate (credential stuffing, see (13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q5)), token endpoint p99, DB pool saturation, and Infinispan cluster size.\n  - Keycloak exposes Micrometer metrics; feed them into the same stack as (20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q1).\n- **Do not fork Keycloak to add a feature.** Use its extension points (SPIs, custom authenticators, event listeners) — a forked IdP is unupgradable, and upgrades are how you get security fixes.\n\nHealth paths, management ports, Argon2 defaults, Infinispan behavior, and Operator workflow are distribution/version/configuration examples. Pin the target Keycloak release and deployment mode, then verify each operational default in its current guide.\n:::"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q6","difficulty":"extra","q":"Keycloak vs a managed IdP vs rolling your own — how do you argue the choice?","a":"Interviewers ask this to hear whether you can weigh [[o:operational cost against control]] instead of naming a favourite.\n\n<table class='cmp'><thead><tr><th></th><th>Keycloak (self-hosted)</th><th>Managed (Auth0/Cognito/Entra)</th><th>Build it yourself</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Cost shape</td><td>Infra + your ops time; flat as users grow</td><td>Per-MAU — cheap early, painful at scale</td><td>Cheapest to start, most expensive to keep</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Data residency</td><td>[[g:You hold the user data]] — often the deciding factor in fintech</td><td>Third party holds it; check the jurisdiction</td><td>You hold it, including the breach</td></tr>\n<tr><td>You now operate</td><td>A clustered stateful service + DB (13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q5)</td><td>Nothing</td><td>Password hashing, MFA, token revocation, federation, and every CVE</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Customisation</td><td>Deep — SPIs, custom flows, own login theme</td><td>Bounded by the vendor</td><td>Unlimited, and entirely your problem</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lock-in</td><td>Low: standard OIDC, exportable realm</td><td>Real: proprietary rules and user store</td><td>None</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n**The defensible position:** [[r:never build your own]] for authentication — it is the least differentiating, highest-blast-radius code you could write.\n\n- Choose **managed** when the team is small and data residency permits.\n- Choose **Keycloak** when residency, per-user cost, or protocol control decide it.\n\nBoth keep you on standard OIDC, so the resource-server code in (13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q2) is [[g:unchanged either way]] — which is exactly why the decision is reversible.\n\n:::deep\n- The migration path is the point worth making: because both speak OIDC, switching IdP changes an `issuer-uri` and a role-claim converter, not your services. What does *not* migrate cleanly is password hashes (formats differ — usually a rehash-on-next-login shim) and the vendor-specific rules engine.\n- Keycloak is [[o:Apache 2.0 with no open-core split]], so there is no feature paywall; the cost is the operations. That matters when comparing against products whose SSO or audit-log tier is priced separately.\n- If you take Keycloak for its features but not its operations, hosted Keycloak (Red Hat build of Keycloak, or a managed provider) is the middle path and a good answer to \"what if we lack an ops team?\".\n- Whatever you choose, the [[g:security model stays yours]]: `aud` validation, immutable `sub` as the user key, short token lifetimes, and authorization decided in your service. An IdP outsources authentication, never authorization.\n\nThe managed-provider names and cost/features in this table are examples, not a universal price or capability contract. Compare the chosen tenant, plan, region, version, residency terms, outage guarantees, and export/migration path before making the build-versus-buy decision.\n:::"}]},{"title":"Secure coding at the boundary","items":[{"id":"13-security-oauth2.secure-coding-at-the-boundary.q1","q":"Injection is more than SQL. Where does untrusted input still reach an interpreter in a modern Spring service?","a":"Parameter binding solved the textbook case and moved the problem elsewhere. Injection survives everywhere a string is [[r:assembled]] rather than bound.\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Surface</th><th>The unsafe shape</th><th>The fix</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>JPQL / native SQL</td><td>string concatenation into the query</td><td>bind parameters; never concatenate values</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dynamic ORDER BY / table name</td><td>identifiers cannot be bound, so people concatenate</td><td>allowlist of column names, map input to constants</td></tr>\n<tr><td>MongoDB filters</td><td>a raw map from the request body becomes the query</td><td>bind typed fields; reject `$` operators from clients</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Shell / process</td><td>`Runtime.exec(\"sh -c \" + arg)`</td><td>pass an argument array, no shell</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Logs</td><td>user text written straight into the log line</td><td>structured fields, encode newlines</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Templates / expressions</td><td>user string evaluated as SpEL or a template</td><td>never evaluate user input as an expression</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"c\">// unsafe: sort is a request parameter concatenated into JPQL</span>\n<span class=\"k\">var</span> q = em.createQuery(<span class=\"s\">\"select o from Order o order by o.\"</span> + sort);\n<span class=\"c\">//</span>\n<span class=\"c\">// safe: identifiers come from a closed set the code owns</span>\n<span class=\"k\">private static final</span> Map&lt;String, String&gt; SORTS =\n        Map.of(<span class=\"s\">\"created\"</span>, <span class=\"s\">\"createdAt\"</span>, <span class=\"s\">\"total\"</span>, <span class=\"s\">\"totalAmount\"</span>);\n<span class=\"k\">var</span> column = SORTS.getOrDefault(sort, <span class=\"s\">\"createdAt\"</span>);   <span class=\"c\">// unknown input degrades, never injects</span></code></pre>\n\n:::tip The rule that generalises\nData is bound, structure is chosen from a set you control. If input decides *structure* — a column, a table, an operator, a class name — it must be mapped through an allowlist.\n:::\n\n:::deep\n- [[o:log injection]] is underrated: a newline in a username lets an attacker forge log lines and poison the incident timeline you will trust later (20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q4).\n- second-order injection stores the payload safely and interpolates it later in a report or admin query. The dangerous moment is the read, not the write.\n- ORMs are not immunity: `@Query` with concatenation, native queries, `Specification` built from raw strings and JPQL `LIKE` patterns are all reachable.\n- validation is not the control here, it is defence in depth. Reject obviously wrong input early (03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q8), then still bind properly.\n- for MongoDB, an untyped `Map` from the body can smuggle `{\"$ne\": null}` into a filter and turn an equality check into \"match anything\" (07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q4).\n:::","difficulty":"core"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.secure-coding-at-the-boundary.q2","q":"A user sets their own role by adding one field to the request body. How does mass assignment happen and how is it closed?","a":"Mass assignment is what happens when the request body is bound straight onto a persistent object. The client controls every field the class exposes, including the ones no form ever shows.\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"c\">// the entity has fields the client must never set</span>\n<span class=\"k\">@PutMapping</span>(<span class=\"s\">\"/users/{id}\"</span>)\nUser <span class=\"f\">update</span>(<span class=\"k\">@PathVariable</span> Long id, <span class=\"k\">@RequestBody</span> User body) {   <span class=\"r\">// entity as the API type</span>\n    body.setId(id);\n    <span class=\"k\">return</span> users.save(body);        <span class=\"r\">// role, status, credit, verified — all writable</span>\n}\n<span class=\"c\">//</span>\n<span class=\"c\">// the DTO is the contract: it can only carry what the caller is allowed to change</span>\n<span class=\"k\">record</span> <span class=\"f\">UpdateProfile</span>(<span class=\"k\">@NotBlank</span> String displayName, <span class=\"k\">@Email</span> String email) { }\n<span class=\"c\">//</span>\n<span class=\"k\">@PutMapping</span>(<span class=\"s\">\"/users/{id}\"</span>)\nUserView <span class=\"f\">update</span>(<span class=\"k\">@PathVariable</span> Long id, <span class=\"k\">@RequestBody</span> <span class=\"k\">@Valid</span> UpdateProfile in) {\n    <span class=\"k\">var</span> user = users.findOwned(id, currentUser());   <span class=\"c\">// authorisation, not just authentication</span>\n    user.rename(in.displayName());\n    <span class=\"k\">return</span> UserView.of(user);\n}</code></pre>\n\n**Three defences, in order of strength:**\n\n- [[g:a dedicated DTO per operation]] — the type system makes the extra field impossible, not merely rejected\n- [[g:explicit mapping]] into the domain object, so adding an entity field never silently widens the API\n- [[g:reject unknown properties]] (`FAIL_ON_UNKNOWN_PROPERTIES`) so a renamed or dropped field fails loudly instead of being ignored\n\n:::warn Response side has the same bug\nSerialising the entity back leaks `passwordHash`, internal flags and the whole object graph. A view model is not ceremony; it is the outbound half of the same control.\n:::\n\n:::deep\n- this is [[r:broken object property level authorisation]] in the API Security Top 10 — the same family as IDOR, one level down: not \"which object\" but \"which field of it\" (13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q3).\n- the field does not have to exist today. Adding `isAdmin` to an entity six months later silently turns a safe endpoint into a privilege escalation.\n- separating the domain model from the JPA entity removes the temptation entirely, and pays for itself in other ways too (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q2).\n- `@JsonIgnore` and `setDisallowedFields` work but are [[o:denylists]]: they protect the fields somebody remembered. The DTO is an allowlist by construction.\n- test it as a security case: post a body containing `role` and assert the stored role is unchanged. That test outlives every reviewer's memory (26-testing-strategy.what-a-test-is-for.q1).\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.secure-coding-at-the-boundary.q3","q":"Which parsing and deserialization features turn \"just reading input\" into remote code execution or a denial of service?","a":"Every parser is an interpreter with a large feature set. The dangerous ones let the *document* decide which code runs or how much memory is allocated.\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>What an attacker gets</th><th>Control</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Java native `Serializable`</td><td>gadget chains &#8594; code execution on readObject</td><td>never accept it from outside; use JSON with a schema</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Polymorphic typing by class name</td><td>instantiates classes you never intended</td><td>no default typing; bind a sealed set of subtypes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>XML external entities (XXE)</td><td>reads local files, reaches internal hosts</td><td>disable DTDs and external entities on every factory</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Deeply nested or huge JSON</td><td>stack overflow, heap exhaustion</td><td>size limits, depth limits, streaming parse</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Compressed uploads</td><td>zip bomb: 1 MB expands to 10 GB</td><td>cap the decompressed size while expanding</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Catastrophic regex</td><td>one request pins a CPU core</td><td>bounded patterns, no user-supplied regex</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"c\">// polymorphic JSON, done safely: the set of types is closed and named by the code</span>\n<span class=\"k\">@JsonTypeInfo</span>(use = Id.NAME, property = <span class=\"s\">\"kind\"</span>)\n<span class=\"k\">@JsonSubTypes</span>({ <span class=\"k\">@Type</span>(value = CardPayment.class, name = <span class=\"s\">\"card\"</span>),\n              <span class=\"k\">@Type</span>(value = BankTransfer.class, name = <span class=\"s\">\"bank\"</span>) })\n<span class=\"k\">sealed interface</span> <span class=\"f\">PaymentInstruction</span> <span class=\"k\">permits</span> CardPayment, BankTransfer { }</code></pre>\n\n**For uploads, validate before you trust the name:**\n\n- decide the type from content, not from the extension or the client's `Content-Type`\n- store outside the web root with a generated name; never execute or serve it back inline\n- cap size at the edge and again while decompressing, and scan when the file will be shared with others\n\n:::warn A file upload is untrusted input that persists\nIt is stored, retrieved, and often rendered later by a different component with different assumptions. That later component is where the exploit lands.\n:::\n\n:::deep\n- the [[r:root cause]] is trusting the document to choose behaviour. Ask of every parser: can the input decide which class is created, which file is read, or how much memory is allocated?\n- responses matter too: serve user files from a separate origin with `Content-Disposition: attachment` and a restrictive `Content-Security-Policy`, so a stored HTML file cannot execute in your domain.\n- limits belong at the edge as well as in code — the proxy rejects a 2 GB body long before the JVM tries to buffer it (14-devops-k8s-best-practices.web-server-networking-observability.q1).\n- log and alert on [[o:parse failures at volume]]: a sudden burst of malformed payloads is usually somebody probing, not a broken client.\n- fuzz the parsing layer of anything that accepts third-party data; it is one of the few places where random input finds real bugs cheaply (26-testing-strategy.what-a-test-is-for.q4).\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.secure-coding-at-the-boundary.q4","q":"Your service fetches a URL the user supplied. What is the full SSRF blast radius and what actually stops it?","a":"SSRF turns your service into the attacker's proxy: it already sits inside the network, already holds credentials, and is already trusted by everything around it.\n\n**Typical entry points:** avatar-from-URL, webhook registration, PDF or link preview generation, XML with external entities, an import job that takes a file URL, and any \"callback URL\" field.\n\n<pre><code>attacker &#8594; POST /import {\"url\": \"http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/…\"}\n              &#8594; your pod fetches it with its own identity\n              &#8594; response body returned to the attacker</code></pre>\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>Strength</th><th>Why it fails alone</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Blocking `localhost` and `127.0.0.1`</td><td>weak</td><td>`0.0.0.0`, IPv6, decimal and octal forms, DNS names that resolve inward</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Allowlist of hosts</td><td>strong</td><td>only workable when destinations are known</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Resolve then validate the IP</td><td>strong</td><td>must re-check after redirects and pin the resolved address</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Egress firewall / proxy</td><td>strongest</td><td>the one control that survives a code mistake</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Disable redirects, cap size and time</td><td>supporting</td><td>limits the damage rather than preventing it</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n:::tip What makes SSRF different from other input bugs\nValidating the string is not enough, because DNS can answer differently the second time. Validate the [[g:resolved address]] and then connect to that address, not to the name again.\n:::\n\n:::deep\n- the cloud metadata endpoint is the classic prize; it hands out instance credentials. Enforce the hardened metadata service and give the workload the narrowest role it can do its job with (14-devops-k8s-best-practices.ci-cd-2026-best-practices.q5).\n- internal services usually have [[r:no authentication]] because \"they are internal\". SSRF is exactly the assumption breaker, which is why service-to-service auth belongs everywhere (13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q2).\n- blind SSRF still works without a response body: timing and DNS lookups leak whether a host exists, which is enough to map a network.\n- treat outbound fetches as a privileged operation: run them from a component with its own restricted egress, not from the service holding your database credentials.\n- webhooks make you the caller by design, so the same controls apply to every delivery attempt, including retries (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q1).\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"}]},{"title":"Protecting the data you store","items":[{"id":"13-security-oauth2.protecting-the-data-you-store.q1","q":"Storage is encrypted, so the data is safe. Which attacks does that actually stop, and which does it not?","a":"Disk-level encryption protects the disk. Almost every incident that matters reaches the data through the application, which sees plaintext by design.\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Stops</th><th>Does not stop</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Full-disk / volume</td><td>a stolen or discarded disk</td><td>any authenticated query, SQL injection, a leaked dump</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Database TDE</td><td>reading data files or backups directly</td><td>a DBA, a compromised app, `SELECT *`</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Field-level (app encrypts)</td><td>DBA access, dumps, most injections</td><td>the service itself once it is compromised</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tokenisation / vault</td><td>the value never enters your system</td><td>abuse of the token where it is accepted</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hashing (passwords)</td><td>reversal after a breach</td><td>weak passwords, credential stuffing</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n**Start from a data classification, not from a technology:**\n\n- public, internal, personal, and regulated (payment, health, government id)\n- for each class: who may read it, how long it is kept, and where it may be copied\n- the strongest control is [[g:not storing it]] — a field nobody stores cannot leak\n\n:::warn \"Encrypted at rest\" is a compliance sentence, not a threat model\nSay which attacker it stops. If the answer is \"somebody who steals the physical disk\", it is true and rarely the attacker you have.\n:::\n\n:::deep\n- field-level encryption has to answer the [[o:query problem]] first: an encrypted column cannot be searched or ranged over. A blind index (a keyed hash of the normalised value) buys equality lookups only, and leaks equality.\n- key management is the real work: a key in the same database is decoration. Use a KMS, keep keys per environment, and rotate with a key id stored next to the ciphertext so old rows stay readable.\n- passwords are not encrypted, they are hashed with a memory-hard function — that distinction still separates engineers in interviews (13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q5).\n- backups and non-production copies inherit the classification. A staging database restored from production is a production data breach waiting for a weaker password.\n- pick the layer by threat: TDE for lost media and auditors, field-level for insiders and dumps, tokenisation to remove the data from scope entirely.\n\n**Independent controls, independent owners:**\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>Answers</th><th>Does not replace</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Encryption at rest</td><td>limits direct media, file, and backup reads</td><td>application authorization or safe handling of plaintext</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Key management</td><td>KMS/HSM custody, separation, rotation, and emergency revocation</td><td>permission to read a domain object</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Application authorization</td><td>principal, tenant, object, field, action, and current-state decisions</td><td>encryption or key custody</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Retention/deletion</td><td>legal basis, expiry, copies, backups, and deletion/crypto-shredding workflow</td><td>confidentiality or tamper evidence</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Immutable audit</td><td>append-only, tamper-evident access and change evidence</td><td>data secrecy or permission to retain personal data</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nTreat a pass in one row as no evidence that the other rows are implemented. Name a control owner, test, retention window, and failure response for each.\n:::","difficulty":"core"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.protecting-the-data-you-store.q2","q":"Where does sensitive data actually leak in a well-built system, and how do you close those paths?","a":"Breaches through the front door make the news; the routine leaks are internal copies nobody classified.\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>What escapes</th><th>Control</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Logs</td><td>request bodies, tokens, card numbers, full stack traces</td><td>structured fields with an allowlist, never log whole payloads</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Traces and metrics</td><td>identifiers as span attributes or labels</td><td>allowlisted baggage, no PII in labels</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Error responses</td><td>internal messages, SQL fragments, hostnames</td><td>a stable error contract, detail behind a trace id</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Support and admin screens</td><td>full records to anyone with the role</td><td>mask by default, reveal on justified action, log the reveal</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Exports and reports</td><td>everything, downloaded to a laptop</td><td>scoped columns, expiring links, audit each export</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Non-production copies</td><td>the whole database, weakly guarded</td><td>anonymise or synthesise on the way out of production</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Third-party tools</td><td>whatever the SDK collects by default</td><td>review defaults, scrub before send</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"c\">// masking belongs in the type, not in each log statement</span>\n<span class=\"k\">record</span> <span class=\"f\">Pan</span>(String value) {\n    <span class=\"k\">public</span> String <span class=\"f\">toString</span>() { <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"s\">\"****\"</span> + value.substring(value.length() - <span class=\"n\">4</span>); }\n}</code></pre>\n\n:::tip The design move that scales\nWrap sensitive values in their own type whose `toString` is already masked. Then a careless log line, an exception message and a debugger all show the masked form by default.\n:::\n\n:::deep\n- [[r:the most common leak is an exception]]: a message containing the offending value, serialised into an error response or shipped to an aggregator. Log the trace id, keep detail server-side (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q2).\n- \"temporary\" debug logging is the second: it is added during an incident, survives the fix, and quietly writes card numbers for months.\n- anonymisation is harder than masking. A dataset with a birth date, a postcode and a gender is usually re-identifiable; sampling and generalising beats replacing names.\n- treat the [[o:log pipeline as a data store]] with its own retention, access control and audit. It frequently holds more sensitive data than the database does (20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q4).\n- add a CI check for the obvious patterns — card-like digit runs and `Authorization:` in log statements — because review misses them at 2am.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.protecting-the-data-you-store.q3","q":"How do retention, deletion requests and an immutable audit trail coexist when the two requirements contradict each other?","a":"\"Delete everything about me\" and \"keep an immutable financial record\" are both real obligations. The resolution is to separate the identity from the transaction.\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Data</th><th>Obligation</th><th>Design</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Identity and contact details</td><td>erasable on request</td><td>one owning service, referenced by id elsewhere</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Financial and tax records</td><td>retain for years</td><td>keep the amounts and the id, drop the personal fields</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Security audit trail</td><td>tamper-evident, retained</td><td>append-only, actor as a stable id, not a name</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Behavioural and analytics data</td><td>minimise and expire</td><td>aggregate early, keep no raw identifiers</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n**What makes erasure achievable at all:**\n\n- personal data lives in [[g:one place]] and everything else stores a reference, so deletion is one operation instead of a manhunt\n- [[g:crypto-shredding]] for stores you cannot rewrite: encrypt per subject and destroy that subject's key, which renders backups and archives unreadable without rewriting them\n- an inventory of copies: search index, cache, data warehouse, exports, partner systems, backups — a deletion that misses these is not a deletion\n\n:::warn Backups are the requirement everyone forgets\nYou cannot rewrite last month's backup. Either commit to a retention window after which backups age out, or make the data unreadable by destroying its key.\n:::\n\n:::deep\n- an audit trail answers \"who did what to which record, and when\" — including [[o:reads of sensitive data]], which is what regulators ask for first and what most systems cannot answer at all.\n- append-only plus a hash chain gives tamper evidence cheaply: each entry carries a hash of the previous one, so silent edits become detectable.\n- separate the audit trail from application logs. Logs are operational, rotate quickly and can be edited; audit records are evidence with their own retention (20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q4).\n- a ledger keeps corrections as new entries rather than updates, which is the same discipline that makes financial records reconstructable (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q3).\n- write the retention rule as code — a scheduled job with its own tests — rather than a paragraph in a policy document nobody executes.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.protecting-the-data-you-store.q4","q":"Most of your running code came from other people. How do you keep dependencies and images from becoming the breach?","a":"A typical service ships a few thousand lines of your code on top of hundreds of megabytes of other people's. Supply chain work is about knowing what you ship and being able to replace it quickly.\n\n**The practices that matter, in order of payoff:**\n\n- [[g:know what is in there]] — generate an SBOM at build time and keep it with the artifact, so answering \"are we affected by this CVE?\" takes minutes rather than a week\n- [[g:scan continuously, not at release]] — a dependency that was clean at build becomes vulnerable the day an advisory is published\n- [[g:upgrade on a cadence]] — small, frequent updates are how you stay able to patch fast; a two-year-old framework turns an urgent fix into a migration project\n- [[g:pin and verify]] — exact versions and digests, lockfiles committed, images pinned by digest so a moved tag cannot swap your base layer\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Surface</th><th>What to check</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Application dependencies</td><td>direct and transitive CVEs, licence, abandoned projects</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Base image</td><td>OS package CVEs, size, non-root user, distroless where possible</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Build pipeline</td><td>who can trigger it, what secrets it can read, provenance of artifacts</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Plugins and actions</td><td>third-party CI steps run with your credentials</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n:::tip The question that reframes the work\nNot \"do we have vulnerabilities?\" — you do. It is \"how long does it take us to ship a patched build?\" Everything else follows from that number.\n:::\n\n:::deep\n- triage by [[o:reachability]], not by CVSS alone: a critical rating in a code path you never call is lower priority than a medium one on your request path. Blind severity queues cause alert fatigue and then get ignored.\n- the build pipeline is production. It holds credentials for every environment, so a compromised CI step is a compromised deployment (14-devops-k8s-best-practices.ci-cd-2026-best-practices.q4).\n- pin third-party CI actions to a commit digest; tags are mutable and have been repointed to malicious code in real incidents.\n- keep one artifact promoted through environments rather than rebuilding per stage, so what you scanned is exactly what you run (14-devops-k8s-best-practices.ci-cd-2026-best-practices.q1).\n- transitive dependencies are the majority of the risk and the hardest to reason about; dependency management that reports the resolved graph is worth more than a scanner nobody reads (03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q3).\n:::","difficulty":"extra"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.protecting-the-data-you-store.q5","q":"A request carries a valid access token. Why can it still be a cross-tenant data breach, and where should the authorization decision live?","a":"A valid signature proves that a trusted issuer minted the token; it does not prove that this principal may access this object, tenant, field, or workflow transition. Treat authentication and authorization as two separate gates.\n\n**Build the effective authorization context from trusted state:**\n\n- principal: `sub`, trusted issuer, intended audience/resource server, client, authentication strength, and token scopes/roles\n- resource: tenant, owner, object state, and relationship loaded from the server-side record—not copied from `tenantId`, `ownerId`, `role`, or `isAdmin` in the request body\n- operation: action, fields being changed, amount, and workflow transition; a `GET` permission is not automatically a permission to export or approve\n- policy: the service’s versioned RBAC/ABAC/relationship rule and any explicit deny or separation-of-duty rule\n\n**Put the invariant at the resource boundary.** The gateway can reject missing tokens and coarse scopes, but the owning service must scope the database query and re-check the object policy before returning or mutating data.\n- derive the tenant from the authenticated context and include it in every read/write predicate\n- allowlist writable fields instead of binding JSON directly to a domain entity\n\nA central policy decision point can reduce duplicated rules, but it adds an availability, cache-freshness, and policy-version boundary; it does not remove the service’s responsibility to bind the decision to the resource.\n\nThe same check must survive caches, search indexes, exports, batch APIs, and asynchronous workers. A worker should carry a bounded, auditable authorization context or re-evaluate policy at execution time; it must not silently replace the user with an unbounded system identity.\n\nStrip client-supplied identity headers and re-bind trusted service identity at every hop.\n\n**Test the access paths:**\n- horizontal access (user A swaps user B’s object) and vertical access (ordinary user invokes an admin action)\n- tenant changes, deleted memberships, and stale policy caches\n- bulk/mass-assignment fields and object enumeration\n- a retried async job\n\nRecord subject, tenant, action, resource, decision, policy version, and request/job id in an access audit event—never the bearer token or other secrets.\n\nOWASP calls the object-id failure mode broken object-level authorization; the durable fix is server-side ownership and action enforcement, not a more convincing JWT.","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.protecting-the-data-you-store.q6","q":"How do you design a webhook or signed/capability URL so a valid signature cannot be replayed or broadened into access?","a":"Treat a webhook and a capability URL as related cryptographic boundaries, not as the same kind of identity.\n\n**For a webhook:** verify the provider’s signature over the exact raw request bytes before parsing or re-serializing; select the expected key and algorithm, use constant-time comparison, and enforce a bounded timestamp window where the provider supports one.\n\nA valid signature still does not prove that the event is current, unique, for the expected account, or safe to apply. So:\n- bind the event to the integration/account, expected object and state, amount or version where relevant, and a provider event id\n- record that id with an atomic uniqueness constraint before the side effect, then make the handler idempotent\n- prefer durable acceptance followed by asynchronous processing over holding the provider connection open; retries must converge to one state\n- give key rotation an explicit old/new overlap and a bounded unknown-key refresh path\n\n**For a capability or presigned URL:** it is a bearer right. Sign the least privilege possible—specific resource, method, content constraints, audience or tenant where supported, and a short expiry.\n\nAssume the URL can leak through browser history, referrers, proxy logs, support tickets, or copy/paste. Use HTTPS, prevent sensitive query logging, restrict upload size/type, and add a server-side one-time or revocation record for high-risk operations.\n\nDo not rely on expiry to make a URL confidential or immediately revocable, and do not treat possession of it as a user session or proof of ownership.\n\nThe final authorization decision still belongs to the resource owner: account binding, current workflow state, idempotency, and allowed transition must be checked before applying the event or serving the object.\n\nTest altered bytes, old timestamps, duplicate event ids, wrong accounts, key rotation, URL method/resource changes, expired and revoked capabilities, and a retry after the side effect.\n\nA signature proves integrity under a key; it does not by itself prove freshness, uniqueness, business authorization, or safe replay.","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.protecting-the-data-you-store.q7","q":"What has to be operationally true for key rotation, session revocation, and audit to remain safe during an IdP or policy outage?","a":"Key rotation and revocation are operational workflows, not a checkbox in the token library. Write down the accepted window and rehearse the failure path.\n\n**Key lifecycle:** inventory every issuer, audience, algorithm, `kid`, JWKS source, signing key, and verifier cache.\n- publish the new verification key before signing with it, keep the old key only for the maximum still-valid token window, and remove it deliberately\n- unknown-`kid` refreshes must be bounded and deduplicated; otherwise one bad token can create a refresh storm\n- an emergency compromise path needs a way to disable a key and invalidate dependent caches without waiting for a normal deployment\n\nNever accept an algorithm, issuer, audience, or JWKS URL selected by the request.\n\n**Session lifecycle:** define whether logout means local session removal, refresh-token-family revocation, device revocation, or a bounded access-token expiry.\n\nRotate refresh tokens and detect reuse where the risk justifies it. Keep session-family and device state so a stolen refresh token cannot mint forever. Do not promise instant revocation if resource servers intentionally cache decisions for hours.\n\n**Outage policy:** high-risk writes—money movement, privilege changes, ownership changes, exports—should fail closed when the required identity or policy proof is unavailable.\n\nA low-risk read may use a tightly bounded stale decision only when the risk owner accepts it, with an expiry, visible telemetry, and an audit reason. Avoid one global fail-open rule.\n\nCircuit breakers and cache TTLs must prevent an IdP/PDP outage from becoming either a security bypass or a retry storm.\n\n**Audit and proof:** send access, key, policy, revocation, and break-glass events to an independently protected sink. Capture subject, issuer, key id, policy version, action, resource, result, reason, request id, and time; omit tokens, refresh secrets, private keys, and sensitive payloads.\n\nTest overlapping rotation, clock skew, JWKS failure, revoked-key replay, logout during an in-flight request, refresh reuse, a second operator session, and recovery/rollback. The system is not operationally safe until those tests show both the intended denial and a recoverable path.\n\n**Bound the degraded mode:** record a maximum stale-key age, maximum stale-policy age, access-token/revocation window, retry budget, and recovery owner.\n\nIf JWKS or the IdP is unavailable, continue only with cached trusted keys inside that policy; do not mint or refresh credentials, and fail closed for high-risk writes. Unknown-`kid` recovery must be single-flight with bounded backoff/negative caching.\n\nA key compromise bypasses normal TTL: disable the key, invalidate dependent caches, revoke sessions, and preserve a separate audit event.","difficulty":"extra"}]}]},"hasVi":true,"stem":"13-security-oauth2","sourceOwners":{"10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q1":"design-review-framework","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q2":"design-review-framework","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q7":"design-review-framework","11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q12":"design-review-framework","11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q21":"design-review-framework","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q3":"traffic-caching-building-blocks","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q4":"traffic-caching-building-blocks","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q5":"traffic-caching-building-blocks","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q6":"traffic-caching-building-blocks","10-system-design-rate-limit.surviving-high-load.q1":"surviving-high-load","10-system-design-rate-limit.surviving-high-load.q2":"surviving-high-load","10-system-design-rate-limit.surviving-high-load.q3":"surviving-high-load","10-system-design-rate-limit.surviving-high-load.q4":"surviving-high-load","10-system-design-rate-limit.survivin